  "command": "make bench-engine-graph",
  "methodology": "Open-loop burst through EngineCore on CUDA bf16, greedy. A case is 128-token prompts, 32 new tokens, block size 16. Every request arrives at t=0. max_batch equals the case batch. A prefill that samples a token stops admission, so the next step decodes that token before the next prefill. A chunk that has not sampled can still share the step. Graphs off is the eager decode forward. Graphs on passes DecodeGraphs into that forward. On a replay, the CPU books the next block table while that graph runs, and seats a waiter into a seat that is already free. The token read is the join: a finish, and a seat that finish frees, are decided after it. The forward for a seat taken there still waits until after that read, and stops once one of those prefills samples. The previous burst, which seated every request in the first step, is bench/results/2026-11-27-engine-overlap.json. There the batch-8 inter-token p99 is that first gap. The pool is one spare page per request past the final length, and at least 32 pages, so a table-width bucket is not clipped by a tiny pool and the run does not preempt. One warmup replay per mode is discarded. It also captures the graphs: the ramp decodes at each bucketed batch, the first of those already spans nine pages, which buckets to 16, and the run never reaches a wider bucket. 5 timed replays follow on the same pool and the same runner. Decode tokens/s is batch / inter-token p50. The requests share each decode step, so that p50 is the step. p50 is nearest-rank over one replay. The reported value is the median of those p50s, with the min and max. The ceiling is decode_step_bytes at context 143, the cached length before the middle decode step, over the median copy in bench/results/2026-10-31-bandwidth.json. Context at the first decode is the prompt length; at the last it is the prompt plus the new tokens, minus one. The trace is one decode step after every request has been prefilled: each earlier step samples at most one new prefill. It is one engine step, with no extra timer sync inside the profiled region. Device-to-host copies are chrome events whose name contains DtoH. The intended copy is the one pageable read of the batch's token ids. Graphs off also copies once to pinned memory: eager decode checks that every new token landed in a reserved page, and that check reads a device bool. The graph path skips the check, because a replay cannot stop on the host. Each trace has one cudaDeviceSynchronize. It is on both paths and does not grow with the batch. A prefill token is read on its own, because finishing it frees a seat before the next admission. Clocks are not locked. The card also drives the display. Host is WSL2. HTTP is not in these numbers.",
